     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** The highest daily dose of sertraline for adults is 200 mg. A 1000 mg dose is five times that. It could cause serotonin syndrome, seizures, or heart rhythm problems. Usual adult doses start at 25\u201350 mg once daily and go up gradually to no more than 200 mg. This could be a typo, such as an extra zero for 100 mg, but I shouldn't guess what was meant.\n\n**2. Orders have to come from a prescriber.** This tool is for clinicians entering medication orders. Since you're not a clinician, you can't authorize a prescription, whatever your role at the hospital. The order needs to come from the patient's treating physician or another licensed prescriber, who can also confirm the right dose.\n\n**What you can do:** If this patient needs sertraline, ask the medicine team's attending physician or resident to review and enter the order. If you got this order from someone else, it would be worth telling the pharmacy or the prescriber that the 1000 mg dose looks like an error, so it doesn't get entered somewhere else.\n\nI'm glad to help a clinician enter a corrected order once it comes from them."
      }
     ],
