     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order, and confirming it doesn't change either problem.\n\n- **The dose:** 1000 mg of sertraline is five times the 200 mg daily maximum. That risk to the patient stays the same no matter who confirms it.\n- **Authority to prescribe:** Prescribing has to come from a licensed clinician who is responsible for this patient's care. Your confirmation can't take the place of that.\n\n**What would work:**\n1. Have the patient's attending physician or another licensed prescriber review and enter the order.\n2. If they think a dose above the usual maximum is clinically needed, they should write down the reason and talk it through with pharmacy first. That safety check exists for this kind of situation.\n3. If the order was meant to be 100 mg (a common typo) or another standard dose, the prescriber can enter that easily.\n\nIf there's a problem getting a clinician to act, for example the patient needs medication urgently and the team can't be reached, I can help you work out who to contact, such as the on-call physician or the pharmacy."
      }
     ],
